This appendix provides detailed visualizations of the feature distributions across generation depths and the resulting embedding structure.

\subsection*{Per-Feature Violin Plots}

Figure~\ref{fig:violin} shows the full distribution of each feature across five generation depths (0--4), pooled across all eight generators ($N$\,=\,37{,}058).
The violin plots confirm the DDR pattern identified in the main text: drifting features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) show progressive narrowing and location shift, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay) maintain approximately constant distributions across depths.
Entropy std exhibits intermediate behavior with modest location shift.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_violin.pdf}
\caption{Feature distributions by generation depth (violin plots, $N$\,=\,37{,}058). Drifting features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio: $|\rho| > 0.60$) show clear location shifts and narrowing with increasing depth. Shape features (entropy max, tail decay: $|\rho| < 0.10$) remain approximately stationary. Quartile lines within each violin indicate median and interquartile range.}
\label{fig:violin}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{DDR Trend Lines}

Figure~\ref{fig:ddr-trends} shows the per-feature depth trend (mean across all samples at each depth) with individual generator traces overlaid.
The consistent downward trajectories for drifting features across all generators confirm that DDR is a universal property of recursive generation, not specific to any single model.
Shape features exhibit flat trajectories regardless of generator, with near-zero $R^2$ for the linear fit.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_ddr_trends.pdf}
\caption{Per-feature depth trends. Black line: pooled mean ($\pm$1 std shading) across all generators. Colored traces: individual generators. Features are labeled by DDR category (drifting, intermediate, stationary). Spearman $\rho$ and linear $R^2$ are annotated per panel.}
\label{fig:ddr-trends}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{UMAP Embedding}

Figure~\ref{fig:umap} shows a 2D UMAP projection of the 7-feature space (7{,}500 stratified samples, StandardScaler normalization, $k$\,=\,30 neighbors, min\_dist\,=\,0.3).
Panel (a) reveals a depth gradient from gen-0 (green) through gen-4 (yellow), with progressive overlap at deeper generations consistent with the convergence that motivates the gen-2+ class collapse.
Panel (b) shows the 3-class view: gen-0 forms a separable cluster, while gen-1 and gen-2+ overlap substantially in feature space, explaining the classification difficulty at this boundary.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_umap.pdf}
\caption{UMAP 2D projection of the 7-feature space ($N$\,=\,7{,}500 stratified sample). (a) Colored by raw generation depth (0--4): a gradient structure emerges, with deeper depths converging. (b) Colored by 3-class label: gen-0 separates clearly; gen-1 and gen-2+ overlap, consistent with the high false-negative rate at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary.}
\label{fig:umap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{Confusion Matrix Visualization}

Figure~\ref{fig:confusion-heatmap} provides a heatmap visualization of the 3-class LOGO confusion matrix (complementing Table~\ref{tab:confusion} in Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).
The row-normalized view in panel (b) highlights: (1) near-perfect gen-0 classification (99.4\% recall), (2) gen-1 confusion primarily with gen-2+ (18.8\%) rather than gen-0 (6.2\%), and (3) gen-2+ misclassification predominantly into gen-1 (23.3\%), consistent with DDR predictions that shallow recursive text has weak fingerprints.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_appendix_confusion.pdf}
\caption{3-class LOGO confusion matrix ($N$\,=\,27{,}529). (a) Raw counts. (b) Row-normalized recall percentages. Gen-0 achieves near-perfect classification; the primary confusion axis is gen-1 $\leftrightarrow$ gen-2+.}
\label{fig:confusion-heatmap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection*{Per-Generator Performance}

Figure~\ref{fig:per-gen} visualizes per-generator LOGO performance.
Panel (a) shows the 8-generator ranking: Pythia models (1B, 2.8B) achieve highest F1, while larger models (Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B) show degradation.
Dashed lines indicate pool-average performance.
The scale effect (larger models $\rightarrow$ lower F1) is consistent with higher-capacity models generating text statistically closer to human writing.

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_appendix_per_gen.pdf}
\caption{Per-generator LOGO performance. (a) 8-generator binary and 3-class F1, sorted by binary F1. Dashed lines: pool average. (b) 6-generator LOGO detail heatmap showing the performance spread across model families.}
\label{fig:per-gen}
\end{figure*}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Depth Classification Performance}
\label{sec:results-classification}

The 7-feature ensemble achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.762 (95\% CI [0.758, 0.766]; $N$\,=\,37{,}058) across eight generators from five families (Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}).
Because gen-0 seeds are shared across generators (\S\ref{sec:generation}), the 3-class metric may carry upward bias from memorized gen-0 patterns; we therefore also report a leak-free binary metric (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+): F1\,=\,0.694, CI [0.688, 0.700], which is unaffected by this sharing.
Per-generator binary F1 ranges from 0.633 (Mistral-7B) to 0.724 (Pythia-2.8B); the primary difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+ (Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).
Larger generators show moderate degradation (Qwen3-8B: 0.692, Mistral-7B: 0.633) relative to the 0.694 average.

\paragraph{Cross-model generalization.}
Cross-model chains (randomly selected generator per step) yield 3-class F1\,=\,0.809 and binary F1\,=\,0.764 under 5-fold CV,\footnote{Cross-model chains use the original five-generator subset (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen); Llama and Mistral were added later for single-generator evaluation.} confirming that depth signal persists when text passes through heterogeneous generators.
The higher cross-model performance relative to single-generator LOGO (3-class: 0.809 vs.\ 0.762) suggests that generator heterogeneity amplifies distributional shifts between depths, making each recursive step more distinct.
Under class balancing, macro F1 reaches 0.831 (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-perclass}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Class} & \textbf{Prec.} & \textbf{Rec.} & \textbf{F1} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
gen-0   & 0.921 & 0.948 & 0.935 & [.923, .946] \\
gen-1   & 0.737 & 0.803 & 0.768 & [.748, .788] \\
gen-2+  & 0.842 & 0.742 & 0.789 & [.768, .809] \\
\midrule
\textbf{Macro} & --- & --- & \textbf{0.831} & [.817, .844] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Cross-model chain per-class performance (class-balanced 5-fold CV, $N$\,=\,2{,}964, 5 generators: Pythia-2.8B/1B, OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B/1.7B). Macro F1\,=\,0.831 under class balancing; under natural class distribution, macro F1\,=\,0.809 (as reported in text). Cross-model chains avoid the gen-0 seed sharing issue present in single-generator LOGO.}
\label{tab:cross-model-perclass}
\end{table}

Leave-one-family-out (LOFO) evaluation yields average binary F1\,=\,0.686 ($\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.8\,pp vs.\ LOGO), confirming transfer across architectural families under the strictest unseen-generator test.
Per-family LOGO performance reveals a scale effect: Pythia generators (1B, 2.8B) achieve the highest binary F1 (0.718--0.724), followed by Qwen (0.691--0.709) and OLMo (0.695), while larger generators Qwen3-8B (0.692) and Mistral-7B (0.633) show degradation.
This pattern is consistent with higher-capacity models generating text closer to the human baseline distribution, compressing the feature margins that DDR exploits.
Appendix~\ref{app:domain} further demonstrates that the signal generalizes across text domains, though with substantial variation (Wikipedia binary F1\,=\,0.789 vs.\ Academic 0.628 at $t{=}0.7$; all domains $>$\,0.73 at $t{=}1.0$).

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Length-matched subsampling yields binary F1\,=\,0.724 (vs.\ 0.770 unmatched; $\Delta$\,=\,$-$0.046); a random-subsample control at equal $N$ (F1\,=\,0.767) confirms the drop reflects deconfounding, not sample-size reduction.
Length accounts for $\approx$6\% of discriminative performance (Appendix~\ref{app:length}).


\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
Pythia-2.8B     & Pythia  & 2.8B & 0.798 & \textbf{0.724} & [.707, .742] \\
Pythia-1B       & Pythia  & 1.0B & \textbf{0.806} & 0.718 & [.700, .735] \\
Qwen3-1.7B     & Qwen    & 1.7B & 0.772 & 0.709 & [.693, .726] \\
Llama-3.2-3B   & Llama   & 3.2B & 0.775 & 0.697 & [.680, .713] \\
OLMo-2-1B      & OLMo    & 1.5B & 0.781 & 0.695 & [.679, .711] \\
Qwen3-8B       & Qwen    & 8.2B & 0.714 & 0.692 & [.674, .708] \\
Qwen3-0.6B     & Qwen    & 0.6B & 0.780 & 0.691 & [.673, .709] \\
Mistral-7B     & Mistral & 7.0B & 0.682 & 0.633 & [.617, .648] \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Average (8-gen LOGO)}} & \textbf{0.762} & \textbf{0.694} & [.688, .700] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{8-generator LOGO macro-F1 for the 7-feature ensemble (bootstrap 95\% CIs, $n$\,=\,1000). Binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) is the primary metric. Note: 3-class F1 may carry upward bias from gen-0 seed sharing across generators; binary F1 is leak-free. Cross-model chains (Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref}) use sample-level CV without this issue.}
\label{tab:logo-results}
\end{table}

Ordinal regression methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) achieve comparable F1 with overlapping CIs, confirming that 3-class granularity provides insufficient resolution for ranking-aware losses.

\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

Features exhibit \emph{differential depth response} (DDR): central-tendency and diversity measures drift monotonically with depth, while shape measures remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution while preserving its functional form.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response: Spearman correlations between individual features and generation depth. Central-tendency and diversity features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show substantial monotonic drift; entropy std ($|\rho|{=}0.17$) occupies an intermediate position; shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled class-balanced Spearman correlations with depth confirm the split (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}):
drifting features show substantial monotonic trends (hapax ratio $\rho = -0.63$, TTR $\rho = -0.61$, entropy mean $\rho = -0.60$),
while shape features remain near-stationary (tail decay $|\rho| = 0.04$); entropy std occupies an intermediate position ($|\rho| = 0.17$).

\paragraph{Distributional shift quantification.}
Two-sample Kolmogorov--Smirnov tests between gen-1 and gen-2+ distributions isolate the recursive depth effect (excluding the initial human$\rightarrow$LLM transition): drifting features exhibit large distributional shifts (entropy mean KS\,=\,0.537, TTR\,=\,0.517, hapax ratio\,=\,0.516; all $p \approx 0$), while shape features show order-of-magnitude smaller shifts (entropy max KS\,=\,0.043, tail decay\,=\,0.052).
All seven features reach statistical significance after Bonferroni correction, but shape-feature KS statistics are ${\sim}10\times$ smaller than drifting features, supporting the ``approximately stationary'' characterization.
Entropy std occupies an intermediate position (KS\,=\,0.285), consistent with its borderline rank correlation.
Levene tests for variance homogeneity reject equal variance for all seven features ($p < 10^{-10}$), indicating that recursive generation alters spread as well as location, though the magnitude of variance change is substantially smaller for shape features (coefficient of variation shift $<$3\% for entropy max vs.\ $>$15\% for hapax ratio).

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
Under 10-fold stratified CV, the full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773) significantly outperforms surface-only (F1\,=\,0.688) and reference-only (F1\,=\,0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}).
Reference-only features yield higher binary F1 (0.833 vs.\ 0.695 for the full ensemble) because surface lexical features introduce noise at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary.
However, surface features are essential for 3-class classification, where they improve gen-0 discrimination by capturing vocabulary diversity patterns absent in reference-model entropy statistics (Full 3-class F1\,=\,0.773 vs.\ Reference-only 0.713).
Permutation importance: hapax ratio (0.35), entropy mean (0.23), TTR (0.18).
Leave-one-feature-out confirms 6 of 7 features contribute positively (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) achieve comparable LOGO F1 (spread 0.013; Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref}), with consistently negative entropy drift, confirming DDR is a property of recursive generation.

\paragraph{Temperature robustness.}
The method operates reliably within $t \in [0.5, 0.9]$: F1 varies by at most 1\,pp from the default $t{=}0.7$ across this range (Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}).
At $t{=}1.0$, performance degrades by ${\sim}6$\,pp as the primary depth signal collapses: entropy mean's Spearman correlation with depth drops from $\rho = -0.60$ to $\rho = -0.002$, indicating that high-temperature sampling injects sufficient token-level randomness to mask the systematic location shift that DDR exploits.

